<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syriza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Coalition of the Radical Left</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Syriza, the Coalition of the Radical Left, is a Greek left-wing party formed as an electoral alliance in 2004 and as a single party in 2012. Led by Alexis Tsipras, it won the elections of January 2015 on a promise to end austerity, but after a referendum rejected the creditors' terms it accepted a third bailout that summer. It governed until 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syriza.jpg" alt="Syriza" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThecentreCZ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_SYRIZA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Its roots lie in the Space for Dialogue for the Unity and Common Action of the Left, formed in 2001 by Greek left-wing organisations with different histories but common ground on issues such as the Kosovo War, privatisations and social rights. Out of it came the Coalition of the Radical Left in January 2004, made up of Synaspismos, the Renewing Communist Ecological Left, the Internationalist Workers Left and other groups. In the legislative election of 2004 it won 241,539 votes, 3.3 per cent, and six seats, all held by members of Synaspismos, the largest partner, which caused tension within the coalition. In 2008 Alexis Tsipras became president of Synaspismos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the election of 6 May 2012 the party polled over 16 per cent and became the second largest in parliament. For the election of 17 June it registered as a single party, because its coalition status would have disqualified it from the 50 bonus seats given to the largest party. It took just under 27 per cent against New Democracy's 29.8 and became the main opposition with 71 seats. A congress in July 2013 decided in principle to dissolve the member parties into a unitary party, and in the European election of 2014 it came first with 26.5 per cent. Its Thessaloniki Programme of 13 September 2014 set out its central demands.</p><p>In the election of 25 January 2015 Syriza won 36.3 per cent and 149 of 300 seats. Tsipras formed a coalition with the Independent Greeks and became prime minister, with Yanis Varoufakis as finance minister. After the government accepted the third memorandum with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s creditors, 25 members of parliament, led by Panagiotis Lafazanis and including the Left Platform, left to form Popular Unity. Tsipras resigned on 20 August 2015 and won the election of 20 September with 145 seats, renewing the coalition. The party lost the election of 2019 to New Democracy and went into opposition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Synaspismos, a democratic socialist party, was the main constituent of the original coalition. The party was founded to unite left-wing and radical left groups and draws on democratic socialism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>, market socialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trotskyism">Trotskyism</a>, with social democrats, Maoists and Marxist-Leninists among its members. It is a member of the Party of the European Left. From 2004 until September 2020 it was represented by three flags, red for socialism, green for ecology and purple for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a>, and afterwards by a star made of the Greek letters Σ and Υ. Among its factions were the Left Platform and the Group of 53, also called Umbrella, founded in mid-2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syriza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
